Subject: [dpadplay] gamescope headless + PipeWire black-frame fix (drmProps + PR #2094)

gamescope 3.16.19 (the 3v1n0 PPA build) has two image-side bugs that block the
dpadplay `:dpad-SteamOS` headless streaming path. This single patch fixes both.
It is applied in the `gamescope-builder` Dockerfile stage and the 4 patched
binaries (gamescope/gamescopereaper/gamescopestream/gamescopectl) are COPY'd
into the vast-vm image over the deb's /usr/games/ binaries.

== Fix 1 (drmProps / headless backend crash) ==

gamescope 3.16.19's CVulkanDevice::createDevice() queries VkPhysicalDeviceDrmPropertiesEXT
via vkGetPhysicalDeviceProperties2 and aborts the headless backend when drmProps.hasPrimary
is false ("physical device has no primary node") or drmProps.hasRender is false
("physical device has no render node").

On some driver/device/container combos (reproduced: UpCloud Helsinki NVIDIA L4,
driver 580.173.02 + 595.58.03, CDI-injected /dev/dri, gamescope-WSI layer loaded)
gamescope's query returns drmProps entirely zeroed -- hasPrimary=false AND
hasRender=false -- even though vulkaninfo (same ICD, same physical device, same
WSI layer) reports hasPrimary=true / hasRender=true (primaryMinor=1 ->
/dev/dri/card1, renderMinor=128 -> /dev/dri/renderD128, both present).
The extension IS advertised (hasDrmProps=true) and the function pointer is
resolved via vkGetInstanceProcAddr (instance is API 1.3), so the query *should*
populate the struct -- but it doesn't. This is a gamescope 3.16.19 query interop
quirk, not a driver/ICD/node issue. (Driver 580 alone does NOT fix it; driver
595 additionally breaks the libGLX ICD for headless -- that's a SEPARATE fix
in entrypoint.sh pointing the ICD at libEGL_nvidia.so.0. See
cloud/docs/UPCLOUD-L4-DEPLOY-2026-07.md blocker #1 + cloud/docs/DRIVER-CUDA-MATRIX.md.)

The headless backend does no KMS scanout, so it genuinely does NOT need a primary
DRM node. The render node IS needed (m_drmRendererFd -> drm syncobjs in
Timeline.cpp), but when the query returns zeros we can fall back to scanning
/dev/dri/renderD* (the CDI-injected nvidia render node) and open it directly.

This part:
  1. Relaxes the headless !drmProps.hasPrimary abort to a warning (continue
     without a primary dev id -- the existing `if (drmProps.hasPrimary)` guard
     already handles hasPrimary=false gracefully for m_drmPrimaryDevId; and
     vulkan_primary_dev_id() is only called from DRMBackend.cpp, which the
     headless backend never uses).
  2. Wraps the render-node acquisition in `if (drmProps.hasRender) {normal
     drmGetDeviceFromDevId path} else {/dev/dri/renderD* scan fallback}` so the
     headless backend can still obtain m_drmRendererFd when the query returns zeros.

Upstream alignment: this matches the intent of the open upstream PR
ValveSoftware/gamescope #2073 ("rendervulkan: do not bail out if the driver
doesn't implement VK_EXT_physical_device_drm"). When #2073 merges + lands in a
3v1n0 build, this part can be dropped.

== Fix 2 (PipeWire black frame + inverted R/B on NVIDIA -- gamescope #1596 / PR #2094) ==

gamescope's `vulkan_acquire_screenshot_texture()` created the screenshot/PipeWire
texture WITHOUT `bSampled`, so no sampled image view was set up for the RGB-to-NV12
conversion shader -- the NVIDIA driver returns BLACK pixels for the PipeWire frame.
gamescope #1596 ("black PipeWire frame during e.g. Steam Remote Play; affects both
Intel and NVIDIA"). The dpadplay UpCloud L4 stream was UNUSABLE because gamescope's
PipeWire node alternated a ~734 KB real frame and a ~6 KB black frame (severe
whole-frame flicker). This is DRIVER-AGNOSTIC (reproduced on driver 580 AND 595;
the earlier "580 menu flicker / #1964" diagnosis was wrong -- #1964 is a desktop
DRM-embedded menu issue, not our headless streaming path).

The fix is gamescope PR #2094 (cmspam, open, not in 3.16.19), split into:
  1. `textureFlags.bSampled = true` (here: `screenshotImageFlags.bSampled = true`)
     in `vulkan_acquire_screenshot_texture()` -- the black-PipeWire-frame fix
     (1 line; safe on all hardware, also fixes Intel).
  2. NVIDIA-proprietary detection: chains `VkPhysicalDeviceDriverProperties` into
     the createDevice() drmProps query, sets `m_bIsNvidiaProprietaryDriver =
     driverProps.driverID == VK_DRIVER_ID_NVIDIA_PROPRIETARY`, and exposes
     `isNvidiaProprietaryDriver()`.
  3. R/B swap: in `paint_pipewire()`, use `DRM_FORMAT_XBGR2101010` as the
     intermediate capture format on NVIDIA proprietary (sampling
     VK_FORMAT_A2R10G10B10 on the NVIDIA proprietary driver returns R/B swapped
     -> the RGB-to-NV12 colour matrix operates on the wrong channels). A #1596
     follow-up confirmed the black fix alone leaves colours R/B-inverted on
     NVIDIA; this fixes it.

PR #2094 patch 2/3 touches the SAME createDevice() drmProps block Fix 1 modifies
-> they coexist cleanly (Fix 1 relaxes the hasPrimary abort + adds the render-node
fallback; PR #2094 adds the driverProps chain + m_bIsNvidiaProprietaryDriver line).

A SEPARATE prerequisite (NOT in this patch): the dpad user must be able to open
/dev/dri/renderD128. Some hosts map the nvidia render node's GID to an unexpected
group (UpCloud: GID 993 = `polkitd` in the container, while dpad is only in
video/audio/input). The entrypoint must ensure dpad is in the render node's owning
group (or chmod the node) -- see the entrypoint render-node permissions block +
cloud/docs/UPCLOUD-L4-DEPLOY-2026-07.md §6a.

Apply on top of the 3v1n0 gamescope 3.16.19 source (`apt-get source gamescope` on
noble with the 3v1n0 PPA + deb-src enabled) with `patch -p1` and rebuild with
`meson setup build && ninja -C build`. Install build/src/{gamescope,gamescopereaper,
gamescopestream,gamescopectl} over the deb's /usr/games/ binaries.

